WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:06.120
One of my earliest memories 
is seeing The Lion King

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.000
and being 
absolutely traumatised

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.120
when Mufasa - spoiler alert - 
gets killed by the stampede.

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:14.480
That had a massive 
profound effect on me

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:17.520
and showed me how much 
a 2D cinema screen 

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:19.520
can make you feel so much.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.360
We did a screening of
Get Out in 2017.

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:24.400
At the end of the film

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:27.720
there was this massive, 
rapturous applause

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:29.720
in the cinema.

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:32.680
It made the hairs on the
back of your neck stand up. 

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:35.040
That is such a special and unique thing

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.160
that only exists in cinemas.

00:00:43.136 --> 00:00:46.736
I think that human beings
are supposed to go to cinema.

00:00:46.736 --> 00:00:47.840
I think it's intrinsic 
to who we are.

00:00:48.171 --> 00:00:49.611
It's something about 
coming together.

00:00:49.773 --> 00:00:52.373
Be it around a fire or in
front of a cinema screen

00:00:52.373 --> 00:00:54.320
is just fundamental
to what it means to be human.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.320
In the very earliest 
days of cinema

00:00:56.600 --> 00:01:00.360
you would go to see films
in really quite strange spaces.

00:01:00.652 --> 00:01:02.692
The archways under 
railway stations

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:05.920
in markets, in shops,
often at fairgrounds.

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:07.045
So it was quite a 
transient thing.

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:08.560
So in the 1920s

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:10.480
the numbers really 
start to go up.

00:01:10.691 --> 00:01:13.251
There's heating, 
comfortable seats

00:01:13.251 --> 00:01:14.720
You start to buy ice cream. 

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:16.883
Suddenly, this is quite
a nice thing to go and do.

00:01:24.252 --> 00:01:27.812
People would risk life
and limb to go and see films.

00:01:27.812 --> 00:01:29.171
So you would have

00:01:29.171 --> 00:01:30.651
air raid sirens going off

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:33.720
fire wardens kind of 
announcing bombs coming

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:36.960
and people would still sit in the cinema.

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:42.760
I think stories

00:01:42.777 --> 00:01:43.937
are one of the things 
that makes us

00:01:44.003 --> 00:01:44.923
 fundamentally human.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.240
And as a neuroscientist

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:47.880
I've always been 
interested in

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.800
how people experience stories.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.400
How do these stories 
affect people

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:53.648
when they're in audiences?

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:06.240
We had people in the cinema
watching this film.

00:02:06.444 --> 00:02:09.364
Then we also had people 
in my lab at UCL

00:02:09.443 --> 00:02:11.443
watching the film by themselves.

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:22.210
What we found is that

00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:23.770
when you're watching 
with an audience

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:25.767
there's much stronger emotional reactions

00:02:25.767 --> 00:02:27.287
than when you're 
watching it alone.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:38.240
When Jasmine and Aladdin 
kiss for the first time

00:02:38.246 --> 00:02:39.886
that is the spike

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:42.360
in electrodermal activity
in the signal 

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:43.725
and electrodermal activity

00:02:43.806 --> 00:02:46.486
is the strongest indicator 
of emotional engagement.

00:03:01.867 --> 00:03:05.947
I went to a screening of 
Singin’ in the Rain

00:03:05.947 --> 00:03:06.811
and I remember

00:03:07.139 --> 00:03:09.219
sitting, going into 
that screening

00:03:09.462 --> 00:03:11.022
feeling really quite miserable.

00:03:11.139 --> 00:03:14.259
And the film came on.
And by halfway through

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:18.140
I just felt absolutely elevated
and so lifted out of myself.

00:03:18.335 --> 00:03:20.095
And it's that feeling

00:03:20.095 --> 00:03:23.095
and it's that moment
that I always come back to.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:24.960
Most of the time when 
you leave the cinema

00:03:25.016 --> 00:03:26.536
I feel, you know

00:03:26.536 --> 00:03:28.055
changed in a way, whether it's

00:03:28.217 --> 00:03:31.217
challenged or whether 
I feel empowered

00:03:31.217 --> 00:03:33.617
or whether I feel 
kind of like energised.

00:03:33.698 --> 00:03:35.418
There's a feeling that it evokes.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:41.560
You're scrolling 
through your phone

00:03:42.021 --> 00:03:44.021
you've got the radio, 
you've got the TV.

00:03:44.021 --> 00:03:46.240
You're constantly being 
shouted at by different voices

00:03:46.701 --> 00:03:49.861
and unless you can immerse
yourself in that one story

00:03:49.861 --> 00:03:51.134
you're not going to be 
transported in the way that

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:53.320
cinema intends to 
transport you.

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:55.680
The lights go out, 
my phone's off

00:03:56.059 --> 00:03:58.499
and I'm just so focused on
what I'm watching.

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:06.072
We live in quite an 
individualistic culture

00:04:06.234 --> 00:04:08.394
and I think whenever 
we can come together,

00:04:08.394 --> 00:04:10.048
whether it's a music concert,
whether it's a boxing match

00:04:10.048 --> 00:04:10.548
or whatever

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:11.680
there’s almost this

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:14.305
ability to move as one, 
to think as one, to feel as one.

00:04:14.305 --> 00:04:15.501
One of the great powers
 of cinema

00:04:15.501 --> 00:04:17.941
is that it's the means by
which we tell each other

00:04:18.022 --> 00:04:19.382
about ourselves

00:04:19.625 --> 00:04:22.463
and it's a means by which we 
develop our empathy muscle

00:04:22.463 --> 00:04:25.298
which is like crucial to becoming better people

00:04:25.298 --> 00:04:26.698
and making the world 
a better place.

00:04:31.168 --> 00:04:32.808
I remember seeing 
Black Panther

00:04:33.133 --> 00:04:35.573
and just the energy in there.

00:04:35.648 --> 00:04:37.168
You're in a room 
full of strangers

00:04:37.183 --> 00:04:38.383
but all of you are 
going through 

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:39.440
the exact same experience 

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:40.320
at the same time.

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:42.680
And I think there’s a
kind of sacred bond there.

00:04:49.822 --> 00:04:51.782
Cinemas haven't 
always been

00:04:52.349 --> 00:04:54.389
totally accessible to everyone.

00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:55.910
And that's a really important thing for us

00:04:56.072 --> 00:04:58.672
to think about in terms 
of people's wellbeing.

00:04:58.793 --> 00:04:59.513
Decades ago

00:04:59.513 --> 00:05:02.432
cinemas were not necessarily
wheelchair accessible.

00:05:02.549 --> 00:05:04.309
Now there are laws in place

00:05:04.309 --> 00:05:05.906
that mean they certainly should be.

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:20.400
Covid really reminded us

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:21.640
of how much we 
need each other

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:23.051
of how much we are 
social animals

00:05:23.213 --> 00:05:24.533
and of how it is different

00:05:24.533 --> 00:05:26.892
watching a film on your own, 
on your laptop

00:05:26.892 --> 00:05:29.412
than going to the cinema and
watching it in darkness

00:05:29.574 --> 00:05:30.694
surrounded by strangers

00:05:30.694 --> 00:05:32.494
who are miraculously 
experiencing the same

00:05:32.494 --> 00:05:33.773
thing as you 
at the same time.

00:05:34.094 --> 00:05:37.054
I suppose that at some point
they might be superseded by

00:05:37.054 --> 00:05:38.730
an even cooler way 
to give us a story

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:40.554
3D holograms or something.

00:05:40.603 --> 00:05:44.043
But the idea that we would 
stop getting together

00:05:44.043 --> 00:05:47.153
as audiences to watch 
a story as a group

00:05:47.153 --> 00:05:48.512
I can't see that 
ever disappearing

00:05:48.512 --> 00:05:49.736
as long as there are humans.

00:05:49.948 --> 00:05:52.028
Cinema is the place where humans go

00:05:52.028 --> 00:05:53.924
to be reminded of what 
it is to be human.

